194: **Known limit:** if the meaning of a whole class of inputs changes, entries for similar but not identical inputs keep replaying the old lesson and slow the correction. Stage 1's reversal measures this cost.
195: 
196: ### 4.6 Forgetting
197: 
198: 1. **Diary forgetting:** entries evicted or superseded, by stage and by lesson group.
199: 2. **Connection forgetting:** for every learnable connection, its net change during the old block and during the new block; the count whose new-block change has the opposite sign ("overwritten connections"), and the share of the old block's changed connections overwritten.
200: 
201: ### 4.7 Persistence
202: 
203: The complete learner state is saved at every checkpoint: connection strengths, eligibility traces and any slow stores the learner keeps (stage 1's learner has a reservoir state), the diary, the reservoir counter and every random-stream state. One lesson's update, receipt and diary admission are published together in one atomic write (temporary file, flush, rename), with SHA-256 hashes in the checkpoint manifest, so a resume never has one without the others. The replay that follows is published in its own atomic write (its update, its log entry and the random-stream states) before the next lesson starts, so a resume restarts at a lesson boundary. Resume goes into a new directory after checking input hashes. Test and validation processes receive only the learner state, never the diary.
263: 
264: Every comparison arm receives **identical online lessons** to the diary arm (same inputs, same pulses, same order). The five replay controls (no old replays, filler, moved timing, no pulse, event-shuffled) are copies of the diary arm's replay log that change **only the replays of old lessons**, meaning entries written in an earlier block. Replays of the current block's own lessons stay exactly as in the diary arm. So through the first block every replay control is in exactly the diary arm's state, and after that the only difference is what happened to the old-lesson replays, which is what the main hypothesis is about. No state is copied between arms after the first block, so later differences are the accumulated effect of each arm's own replay changes, not an isolated intervention from identical starts. The dry run checks that the first-block snapshot of each replay control equals the diary arm's, bit for bit. Stage 2 does this with a fixed tape. In stage 1 the diary arm makes its own choices, as in the original task, and each added arm is **yoked** to them: it receives the diary arm's chosen input and pulse at every trial instead of choosing. The task's four original arms keep their own choices and serve only to reproduce the original marks. The case-recall arms and the two learners are run in stage 2 as descriptive results and are decisive only in stage 3.
265: 
266: | Arm | What it is | What it rules out |
267: |---|---|---|
268: | **Diary** | Online lessons plus one replay per lesson (section 4) | (the system under test) |
269: | **No diary** | Online lessons only. Its first-block learning also lacks the diary's replays, so diary versus no diary mixes better learning with better keeping; the replay controls separate the two | Baseline; the Volume 2 arm |
270: | **No old replays** | Identical to the diary arm, except that every old-lesson replay is skipped: no step at all. It matches the diary arm bit for bit through the first block, gets the same lessons and the same current-block replays, and after the first block carries its own state forward (nothing is copied from the diary arm) | The reference for forgetting and for filler damage: it differs from the diary only by the missing old-lesson replays |
271: | **Filler replay** | Each old-lesson replay is replaced by one step on a non-chess filler pairing (the transfer protocol's filler trials; in stage 1, a fixed set of arbitrary pairings never tested) | Rival 2a |
272: | **Moved timing** | Copies the diary arm's replay log from the same run. Every replay is kept. For each block (stage 1: each item block and the reversal; stage 2: A and B), the replays made during that block of entries from earlier blocks are moved, in the same order, to the end of the block before it, after that block's test snapshot and before the next block starts; replays of the current block's own entries stay where they were | Rival 2b |
273: | **No pulse** | Each old-lesson replay keeps its input and time but delivers no external teaching pulse | Rival 3 |
274: | **Event-shuffled** | Within each block, the pulses of that block's old-lesson replays are randomly permuted across those replays, so each keeps its input and time but gets another old-lesson replay's pulse. Pulse counts, amplitudes and timing per block are unchanged | Re-exposure with wrong verdicts |
275: | **Mixed order** (stage 2 only) | All A and B lessons in one shuffled order, with one filler step per lesson so its update count matches the diary arm, no diary; scored only at the end | A feasibility witness only: success shows a joint setting is reachable; failure means only that this schedule failed |
276: | **Learning off** | Same lessons, no connection changes | Floor |
277: | **Feedback cut** (descriptive) | Diary arm with output-to-dopamine connections silenced during replay passes only | Whether replay amplifies existing preferences |
278: | **Case recall** | At test, scores each candidate from stored lessons (exact specification below), in two versions: the final diary, and the **full history** of every lesson | Rival 5 |
279: | **Raw-feature learner with diary** | Simple learner (below) on raw photoreceptor input, same lessons, same diary and replay | Rival 6 |
280: | **Random-feature learner with diary** | Same learner on fixed random nonlinear features | Rivals 5 and 6 |
281: 
282: **Case recall, exact rule.** Keys, all fixed before data with the `features` stream: (a) raw input vector; (b) Kenyon-cell rates: the rates of the untrained learner in one forward pass on the input, with no learning and no dopamine; (c) random features: a projection z = Wx, where W has one row per Kenyon cell with independent standard normal entries drawn from the `features` stream, row by row. Keep the k units with the largest z (ties go to the lower unit index); the kept units carry max(z, 0) and all others are zero. k is the mean number of active Kenyon cells per development input at the untrained state, rounded half up, computed once from the development inputs before any data. Each key is scaled to unit length; distance is Euclidean. A candidate's score is the inverse-distance-weighted mean of the pulse signs (+1 reward, -1 punishment) of its 3 nearest stored lessons, weight 1 / (d + 10??); if any stored lesson is at distance below 10?��, the score is the mean sign over those exact matches only. Ties and tie tolerance follow the fly's scoring. The bar is the best of the three keys in each version, chosen per stage on the final result: this favours the control.
283: 
284: **Simple learners, exact rule.** Score = w � f(x), with w starting at zero. On each lesson or replay with pulse sign s: w  w + s f(x) / (?f(x)?� + 10??). f is the raw input (the dense vector of Eyes v2 current on each photoreceptor, zero where the stimulus is absent) or the random features above. There is no learning rate to tune: because w starts at zero and every update would be multiplied by the same rate, a rate only rescales all scores and cannot change any ranking.
285: 
344: 
345: ### 7.5 Stage 3: transfer and combination, on the fly's own choices
346: 
347: Use [Phase B and Phase C of the transfer protocol](../research-goals/transfer-and-combination-protocol-2026-11-03.md) unchanged, on the fly's own choices. For each fly arm of the protocol (Phase-A, fresh, shuffled, single-idea and two-idea flies) add a version with a diary, and add the case-recall arms (diary and full history) and both learners with the diary. Each diary belongs to one fly and travels with its learner state across phases; its capacity is one quarter of that fly's total planned lessons across all its phases, fixed before data. The protocol's filler trials carry feedback the fly receives, so they are lessons and enter the diary like any other. The fly is always tested with the diary removed. Each locked set is opened once.
348: 
349: **Who is trained on what.** Each fly arm chooses for itself, as its protocol specifies, so fly arms see different lessons. Each case-recall arm and each learner with the diary is fed the complete receipt stream of the fly it is compared with, in the same order: the same lessons and pulses, plus that fly's replays, taken from its receipts. The protocol's missing-idea and filler conditions are kept as the protocol defines them, so a control that lacks an idea is never given the omitted lessons. For the central attribution test (the full fly against the case-recall and learner arms), the learner arms are matched to the evaluated full fly's actual lessons and replay tape, so the learner is the only difference. The auditor checks that each matched arm's receipt stream equals its fly's, byte for byte.
350: 
351: A **reasoning claim for the fly plus diary** at Phase C step 2 (three ideas) requires, in all three runs, with every audit passed:
352: 
353: 1. the protocol's own pass scores;
354: 2. the same scores on the offset-cancelled pairs;
355: 3. at least 6 more locked pairs than each added arm, bootstrap lower limit above 0 (the protocol's margin);
356: 4. the all-legal measure on the locked boards, scored as in stage 2 (fixed denominator, worst case for the diary on unknown boards), above learning off, the case-recall arms and both learners, each with bootstrap lower limit above 0.
357: 
358: Its wording is then limited to: the fly with a disclosed replay diary combined three separately taught chess ideas on unfamiliar problems better than the listed case-recall and simple-learner controls. A case classifier not among those controls is not ruled out. Failing any of 1 to 4 leaves reasoning unsupported, whatever stages 1 and 2 showed.
359: 
360: ## 8. When the design fails
361: 
362: 1. **Leak:** a diary field cannot be rebuilt from receipts; invisible details change the replay tape; a validation or test input appears in the diary; a test process loads the diary. The run is invalid.
363: 2. **Nothing to keep:** the fly does not acquire the first lessons. Uninterpretable.
364: 3. **Prerequisite not met:** the stage's forgetting prerequisite fails: section 7.3 for stage 1, and for stage 2 the no-old-replays arm (which learned A exactly as the diary arm did) must forget during B, or the stage says nothing about the diary. The no-diary arm's result is reported beside it, but it does not decide the prerequisite.
365: 4. **Extra practice explains it:** the diary does not beat filler replay (unless filler is shown damaging) or the moved-timing arm.
366: 5. **The verdict is not needed:** the diary does not beat the no-pulse arm.
367: 6. **Refresh only:** the diary fails at the low-point snapshot.
368: 7. **Stability blocks learning or correction:** stage 2 condition 5 fails, or stage 1's reversed item fails.
369: 8. **Does not work under pressure:** no benefit at capacity one quarter.
16:- **Bootstrap lower limit:** resample whole games with replacement 10,000 times (seed 20261103) and take the 2.5% point of the difference in correct pairs. Implemented as `pairGate` in [transfer-evaluation-v1.mjs](../../../.codebase/src/neural-learning/transfer-evaluation-v1.mjs).
17:- **Run:** one complete training and test with one puzzle order and one choice seed. Three runs are not three flies.
25:5. **Equal practice:** every arm gets the same number of feedback trials in the same order, with the same seeds. An arm that skips a training block gets the same number of **filler trials** instead: the non-chess arbitrary-pairing trials of the 2026-11-01 pilot, which carry feedback but no chess fact.
119:**Arms:** Full fly (ideas 1 and 2); idea-1-only fly (480 filler trials in place of idea 2); idea-2-only fly (filler in place of idea 1); fresh fly (960 filler trials); shuffled-feedback fly; plasticity-off fly; simple learner; similar-board recall; exact-recall store; and the **fixed-sum combiner**, which adds the idea-1-only fly's and idea-2-only fly's move scores with a fixed rule. The fixed-sum combiner tests the review's warning that a hand-written rule could combine two answers without the fly learning to combine.
141:**Arms:** Full fly; each two-idea fly (filler in place of the missing idea); each single-idea fly; fresh fly; shuffled-feedback fly; plasticity-off fly; simple learner; similar-board recall; exact-recall store; fixed-sum combiner of the three single-idea flies.

