documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:298:5. **Not extra practice:** in every seed, the diary keeps at least as many items correct at each test after new learning as the five replay controls. In every seed where the no-old-replays arm showed damage after new learning (at the end of learning or after the reversal), the diary also beats each of the five replay controls at that test: more items correct, or, with equal counts, a higher median margin ratio. A seed where the no-old-replays arm showed no such damage cannot show a benefit and is not required to. If in a seed the filler arm keeps fewer items than the no-old-replays arm, filler damaged learning there, and the filler comparison is reported as uninformative for that seed.
documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:332:7. **Beats the practice controls:** more A pairs correct after B than no diary, no old replays, filler, moved timing, no pulse and event-shuffled, each with bootstrap lower limit above 0. If the filler arm ends B with fewer A pairs than the no-old-replays arm (lower limit above 0), filler damaged learning; that comparison is then reported as uninformative and the others decide. Because the replay controls start B in exactly the diary arm's state, those five comparisons measure keeping, not learning.
