**New findings**

1. **required-BUILD — Stage 3 leaves the lookup store’s contents ambiguous.** The new rule feeds replay events to both case-recall controls, whose stores are defined as the final diary and the full lesson history. If repeated replays enter the history as extra cases, one lesson can fill all three nearest-neighbor slots and change whether the fly beats the control. Specify that the diary lookup uses the evaluated fly’s final diary, the full-history lookup uses one entry per original lesson receipt, and the simple learners update on both original lessons and replays. [Design:278](/C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:278), [design:282](/C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:282), [design:349](/C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:349)
2. **required-LAUNCH — The same seed can still yield different random features.** Box–Muller gives two normal values per draw, and the spec leaves their consumption order open. Two implementations can produce different control scores from the same seed. Pin which value comes first and whether the second is reused, then check the first few outputs for one registered seed. [Design:207](/C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:207)
